    "analysis": {
      "fix": "Refactor getOrderHistory to flatten all product IDs from the retrieved orders into a single array and fetch them in one batch using the existing db.getProductsByIds function. Then map the retrieved products back to their respective orders in memory instead of awaiting individual network round trips in a loop.",
      "evidence": [
        "getOrderHistory avg ms: 109.4",
        "db.getProductById calls: 156894",
        "db.getProductById calls per getOrderHistory() call: 18",
        "db.getProductById avg ms: 5.7",
        "db.getProductById time spent ms: 899827"
      ],
      "headline": "getOrderHistory is slow because it suffers from the sequential N+1 query problem inside a nested loop.",
      "confidence": "high",
      "fixed_code": "exports.getOrderHistory = async (userId) => {\n  const orders = await db.getOrdersForUser(userId);\n  const allProductIds = orders.flatMap(order => order.productIds);\n  const products = await db.getProductsByIds(allProductIds);\n  const productMap = new Map(products.map(p => [p.id, p]));\n  return orders.map(order => ({\n    ...order,\n    items: order.productIds.map(id => productMap.get(id))\n  }));\n};",
      "root_cause": "The function getOrderHistory calls db.getOrdersForUser(userId) once (averaging 1.4 calls per execution), returning 4 orders, and then iterates through each order and its productIds array to call db.getProductById(productId) sequentially. Based on the telemetry, this results in an average of 18 calls to db.getProductById per getOrderHistory call, spending an average of 5.7 ms per call and accumulating 899,827 ms of total time across 156,894 calls. Because these product fetch operations are awaited sequentially inside a nested loop rather than batched using the existing db.getProductsByIds function, latency compounds linearly with the number of products.",
      "expected_impact": "By batching the product lookups, the 18 sequential round trips to getProductById are replaced with a single round trip to getProductsByIds. With db.getOrdersForUser taking 5.2 ms and a batched product fetch taking approximately 6.5 ms, the estimated latency is 11.7 ms."
